{"title":"Dimension","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>In <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathematics\" title=\"Mathematics\">mathematics</a>, <b>dimensions</b> are parameters used to describe the position and characteristics of an object in <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Space\" title=\"Space\">space</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Basic_Dimensions\">Basic Dimensions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Dimension&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Basic Dimensions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A 0-dimensional object is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Point\" title=\"Point\">point</a>, which has no <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Length\" title=\"Length\">length</a>, height, or <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Depth&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Depth (page does not exist)\">depth</a>.\n</p><p>A 1-dimensional object is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Line\" title=\"Line\">line</a>, or <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Line_segment\" title=\"Line segment\">line segment</a>, which has length, but no other characteristics.\n</p><p>A 2-dimensional object has length and height, but no depth. Examples of 2D objects are <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Plane\" title=\"Plane\">planes</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polygon\" title=\"Polygon\">polygons</a>.\n</p><p>A 3-dimensional object has length, height, and depth. Examples of 3D objects are <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cube_(geometry)\" title=\"Cube (geometry)\"> cubes</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sphere\" title=\"Sphere\">spheres</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Other_Dimensions\">Other Dimensions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Dimension&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Other Dimensions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Physicists often view the 4th dimension as <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Time\" title=\"Time\">time</a>.  This view is not necessary in mathematics: mathematicians can calculate and prove a wide variety of facts about higher-dimensional objects (such as the 4-D <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Hypercube\" title=\"Hypercube\">hypercube</a> or <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Hypersphere\" title=\"Hypersphere\">hypersphere</a>), even if these objects cannot exist physically in our (apparently) 3-D universe.\n<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=N-dimensional&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"N-dimensional (page does not exist)\">N-dimensional</a> math is helpful in some fields, such as linear algebra.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Dimension&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=General_relativity\" title=\"General relativity\">General relativity</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Special_relativity\" title=\"Special relativity\">Special relativity</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Albert_Einstein\" title=\"Albert Einstein\">Albert Einstein</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Dimension&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018073926\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.015 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.037 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 30/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 26